 "notes": [
  "The parts list matched the 'analog sensors' to ntc-temperature-sensor, but the circuit image shows force-sensitive resistors (FSRs), so the catalogue type fsr is used. The image shows 3 FSR legs; only the 2 active legs are modelled.",
  "FSR legs are shown with red/blue leg extensions; they are modelled as FSR leads inserted directly into the breadboard rather than as separate jumper wires.",
  "Breadboard hole names follow the catalogue (top half 't' rows a-e, bottom half 'b' rows f-j). Image rows j..f (top half) map to a..e and image rows e..a (bottom half) map to f..j. Only strip membership matters.",
  "Rail hole numbers are estimated from the image. tp/tn are the top + and - rails, and bp/bn are the bottom + and - rails. 5V goes to the + rail and GND to the - rail.",
  "Each of the two sensors forms a divider. FSR goes from the + rail to a node, and a 10K resistor goes from that node to the - rail. In the image, the node at column 16 goes to A0 and the node at column 6 goes to A1.",
  "Pushbutton sits across the centre gap with legs in columns 5 and 7. Side 1 (left column, 5t/5b) is on the 5V strip and side 2 (right column, 7t/7b) is on pin 2 and the 10K pull-down. The bottom legs are not wired to anything else.",
  "The pull-down resistor goes from column 7 (pin 2 strip) to column 10 (GND strip via the black jumper).",
  "Resistors are 10K, so value=10000."
 ],
